it's great to welcome to the program  today Hannah gurmann who is a professor  of US history and American Studies at  New York University's Gallatin school of  individualized study she has been  working on the issue of whistleblowers  and leakers in the United States and  around the world and first of all thanks  for joining me a Hannah really  appreciate it  thanks for having me so we get a lot of  our understanding of whistleblowers and  leakers from reports in the media in the  United States and that those reports may  not always completely precisely convey  the difference between a whistleblower  and a leaker so let's maybe start there  how should the audience be thinking  about the difference between the two  terms right well I'm glad you asked that  question because I think part of what  makes it really difficult to even begin  a conversation about whistleblowing is  lack of consensus on the terms and a  tendency to conflate we whistleblowing  and leaking so leaking is endemic to the  state right so politicians will on an  everyday basis leaked to the media  however when something happens a  disclosure that they don't like they'll  use the term leaked often as a way to  discredit that disclosure on the other  hand whistleblowing is what happens when  somebody discloses information to the  public with an eye towards the public  interest with an eye towards changing  policy as a form of protest as a form of  dissent so very different from the  everyday tactics and political  maneuvering that happens among senior  policymakers on an everyday basis yes so  I mean that sounds great in terms of a  distinction but I think people will  immediately realize that much of that is  open to interpretation in terms of  whether you so I guess one thing that  comes to mind to me is when you call  something a leak you're implying the  focus should be on the fact that  information was released that shouldn't  have been as the sort of most important  characteristic when you call something  whistleblowing  you're shifting the focus not to  how we learned about it but to the  importance of the content in what was  released and there's certainly some  subjectivity there yes I think that's a  key point and so this is a political  terrain how one identify something as a  leak or as a form of whistleblowing and  to your first point it really is the  state and those who oppose such  disclosures that will pretty much  inevitably say this is a form of  unauthorized disclosure because when  information is classified secret it is  illegal to disclose whistleblowers take  it upon themselves to disclose that  information because they believe that it  is important and in the public interest  for people to know so yes this is going  to be a constant struggle there's no  science it is a political act to  identify somebody as a whistleblower and  it is important to say that the content  matters right I mean when Daniel  Ellsberg who disclosed the Pentagon  Papers in 1971 did what he did  the Supreme Court decided that the  content was important enough that the  American people and the world needed to  know it's a complicated case because the  press was allowed to publish although  Daniel Ellsberg's case ended in a  mistrial so we'll never actually know  how the courts would have decided there  was somewhere I read I don't remember  exactly where which attempted to draw a  distinction between leaking and  whistleblowing on the grounds that  whistleblowing is exposing activity that  is illegal but that actually seems a bit  a bit too simplistic for me I mean what  you could say well Chelsea Manning  exposed crimes that were being committed  allegedly committed by members of the  American military Edward Snowden exposed  practices that while maybe unsavory  maybe not productive to the national  security of the United States as our  intelligence agencies might claim they  were legal sort of by statute I mean we  decide what's legal illegal and what  isn't legal so I'm wondering whether the  lagaa  of what's exposed is relevant or whether  it's a distraction or somewhere in  between right it's probably somewhere in  between because there are whistleblowers  who do expose gross illegalities waste  fraud abuse and those things fall more  classically into a whistleblowing  paradigm as it's developed within a body  of statutory and regulatory law but a  lot of the most important disclosures  made by national security whistleblowers  as you say would not be considered legal  in well fundamentally because the very  act of disclosing is illegal and then  also as you mentioned because the  national security state is so powerful  it has an army of lawyers that make a  body of law to justify its actions so I  do think it can be a slippery slope well  you have really great lawyers who are  working on these issues the law can't  and shouldn't be the only terrain on  which we're talking and thinking about  and defending whistleblowers and I do  support the idea of thinking about  whistleblowers in the longer tradition  of civil disobedience while some are  clearly obeying the law others choose to  break the law in the name of a higher  purpose so there's a big connection  between how whistleblowers and leakers  are treated by an administration or a  government and issues of press freedom  and defending the the press I want to  think back a little bit to prior  administrations one of my I had many  criticisms from the left of the Obama  administration we could talk about the  drone program for example not what we're  talking about today but there are there  are many criticisms of the Obama  administration in terms of how they  dealt with whistleblowers and leakers do  you have any overall or overarching  themes that you've identified in terms  of how Democratic versus Republican  administrations deal with whistleblowers  and leakers or is there not a material  difference between the two  that's a great question I think this has  less to do with distinctions between  Republicans and Democrats than with  executive power and so you have under  the Carter Administration for example a  huge ramping up of the use of  non-disclosure agreements many cases  that went to court to censor national  security employees when were in books  during the period so Carter a Democrat  somebody who you maybe you might imagine  had different views on national security  but still very much clamping down on the  First Amendment rights of national  security employees and very much  denying whistleblower status to a whole  array of individuals that should be  considered whistleblowers so while there  have been some major crackdowns that  have happened  under Republicans Nixon obviously  charging Ellsberg under the Espionage  Act I I think and I think overall this  is not a partisan distinction but much  more one of presidential power and  executive power can you talk a little  bit you talked about whistleblower  status can you talk about what that  means and how this relates to how  individuals who release information are  handled both by by the government and  the justice system sure and well  whistleblower status is denied to most  individuals to disclose to pretty much  anybody who just closes classified  information or anything related to  national security so there's a very  narrow scope in which anybody who does  kind of try to report these wrongs would  be considered a whistleblower and that  through a series of internal channels  that have been developed in recent years  and so Obama for example said you know  Snowden could have used internal  channels but  history of those channels is is is not  one that should give us much confidence  in them there's a lot of evidence that  they're used that they're not only  ineffective but that they become modes  of retaliation against people who try to  disclose wrongdoing so anybody who goes  outside of those channels is not  officially by the state considering a  whistleblower and so they don't really  have a lot to stand on in a court of law  in addition if their case were to go to  trial they are not in a position to  defend themselves using the classified  evidence in which they were trying to  expose gross wrongdoing so their hands  are pretty tied and it's really hard to  kind of imagine how this would play out  in a formal system of justice in any way  that serves justice to either the  whistleblower or to the public who's  benefiting from those disclosures so let  me make sure I understand that because  that seems like a key point if you are  indicted and charged with crimes related  to information that you made public that  had classified or other protected status  you might imagine that it would be  influential to a jury for example to  hear the content of what it was that you  were trying to expose it might be crimes  it might be obvious gross wrongdoing as  you suggest but you would not be able to  use that as a defense because of the way  that that information is itself  classified which effectively ties your  hands in terms of how to strongly defend  yourself in a court of law  exactly yeah and and there is no in  other words there is no Public Interest  defense that would be a defense where  you would be able to use that material  to show that yes well you broke the law  you did so in the name of the public  interest and you would try to prove that  what you had done was in the public  interest right now there is no such  thing there are  a body of lawyers that are working to  develop a public interest defense at an  international scale but it's very hard  to imagine that that would happen  anytime soon so let's talk now or tie  this in to press freedom in the sort of  tail end of our conversation journalists  reporters analysts all sorts of  different people that work in media  regularly are in touch with sources of  different kinds sometimes their sources  that might be considered leakers when we  hear something that went on in the  hallway outside of the Oval Office for  example sometimes the sources might at  least colloquially be called  whistleblowers even if they don't have  that formal status can you kind of  connect government policy towards  whistleblowers and leakers to the  fundamental issue of Press Freedom and  the duty to report for journalists and  reporters yes I think it cannot be  understated how important whistleblowers  are to a Free Press  it's really a symbiotic relationship and  in the course of researching the history  of national security whistleblowing this  theme has just been confirmed time and  again you you can't have the kind of  investigative reporting that one needs  on the national security state without  insiders and without people who are  willing to expose the kinds of secrets  that we are not otherwise privy to so if  you don't have whistleblowers you don't  really have a free press and it's a  complicated history but we live in a  situation in which the press has the  right to publish but the people who are  disclosing the information don't have  the right to disclose it and that is an  insane paradox that has to be somehow  resolved otherwise we really don't have  First Amendment rights in this country  for the millions of people who do have  access to classified information yet  sort of strikes me as the the  circumstances that at some point in  different states existed with marijuana  which was it's not illegal to have some  of it but you're not allowed to buy it  and you're not  to grow it so then how did you obtain it  in the first place and it seems that a  lot of journalists and reporters are put  in this very strange situation like that  yeah yeah an increasingly journalists  who had previously thought that this  kind of irony benefited them that they  were safe increasingly see that the  sanctions that have been put up against  whistleblowers are increasingly  threatening the press as well can you  talk about how that works because you're  right that there was this idea that once  you had the information even if the  person who gave it to you didn't do so  legally you now are protected or  shielded in some way as a member of the  media that that's not always the case  yeah I mean well there have been some  famous instances considering for example  Judy Miller from the New York Times when  she refused to disclose her source she  was threatened with contempt of court  and did in fact go to jail so that's one  aspect of it and also increasingly in  the Trump administration you have even  more direct threats about press  censorship and the threat of actually  taking journalists to court and  potentially trying them for publishing  classified information absolutely  important issue I'm really glad that  professor Hannah gurmann US history and  American studies professor at New York  University was able to join us and talk  about this Hannah really appreciate your  time today thanks for having me

